g closed source.\n- If you distribute a product that links to it or bundles it, the combined work generally has to be released under GPL-3.0, source included. That covers static or dynamic linking, and it covers bundling it into an installer, binary, or container image you hand to customers.\n- GPL-3.0 also has anti-lock-in terms. If you ship it in a consumer device, you'd have to allow users to install modified versions. It includes a patent license grant as well.\n- It's less of a problem if the product is a hosted service that users never receive a copy of, because GPL-3.0 doesn't trigger on network use. That's the AGPL's rule. Check that `somelib` isn't actually AGPL, and be careful if you ever hand out an on-prem or client-side build.\n\n**Your options, roughly in order of preference:**\n1. Replace it with a permissively licensed alternative (MIT, BSD, Apache-2.0).\n2. Ask the authors for a commercial or dual license. Many GPL projects offer one.\n3. Isolate it as a separate program that you only call at arm's length, such as a CLI over a pipe. This is legally subtle, so get counsel's opinion first.\n4. Open-source your product under GPL-3.0 if that's acceptable to you.\n\n**The other two are fine for closed source:**\n- `requests` (Apache-2.0) is permissive. You need to include the license text and any NOTICE file, and you must state changes if you modify it.\n- `left-pad` (MIT) is permissive. You need to include the copyright and license notice.\n\n**Other things to check before you ship:**\n- **Transitive dependencies.** deps.txt lists only the top-level packages. `requests` pulls in `urllib3`, `idna`, `certifi` and others. `certifi` is MPL-2.0, which is file-level copyleft and generally fine unmodified. Run a full scan, for example `pip-licenses` against the installed environment, so a GPL package doesn't hide further down.\n- **Missing license data.** The licenses in this file look hand-entered. Verify them against each package's actual metadata and the version you pin.\n- **Attribution.** Ship a third-party notices file covering the Apache and MIT dependencies.\n\nThis is a general read, not legal advice. For the GPL question in particular, have counsel confirm before release.\n\nIf you'd like, I can run a full transitive license scan of the actual environment.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}